{"subjectId":"sql","id":"top-n-per-group","title":"Top-N per Group & Department Max","group":"Interview Prep","order":57,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">“Highest-paid employee in each department”, “latest order per customer”, “top 2 products per category” — one pattern with a few variants.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-crown\"></i>Department max: three ways</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- 1. window function\nSELECT department, name, salary FROM (\n    SELECT d.name AS department, e.name, e.salary,\n           RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\n) WHERE rnk = 1\nORDER BY department;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>department  | name  | salary\n------------+-------+-------\nEngineering | Asha  | 150000\nHR          | Sara  | 85000\nSales       | Priya | 110000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- 2. join to the per-group max\nSELECT d.name AS department, e.name, e.salary\nFROM employees e\nJOIN (SELECT dept_id, MAX(salary) AS max_sal FROM employees GROUP BY dept_id) m\n  ON m.dept_id = e.dept_id AND m.max_sal = e.salary\nJOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nORDER BY department;\n\n-- 3. correlated subquery\nSELECT e.dept_id, e.name, e.salary\nFROM employees e\nWHERE e.salary = (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees i WHERE i.dept_id = e.dept_id)\nORDER BY e.dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>department  | name  | salary\n------------+-------+-------\nEngineering | Asha  | 150000\nHR          | Sara  | 85000\nSales       | Priya | 110000\n\ndept_id | name  | salary\n--------+-------+-------\n1       | Asha  | 150000\n2       | Priya | 110000\n3       | Sara  | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>All three return <strong>ties</strong> (both rows if two people share the max). Use <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> instead of <code>RANK</code> when exactly one row per group is required, with a deterministic tie-breaker.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Top 2 products by revenue per category</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH product_revenue AS (\n    SELECT p.category, p.name, COALESCE(SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price), 0) AS revenue\n    FROM products p\n    LEFT JOIN order_items oi ON oi.product_id = p.product_id\n    GROUP BY p.product_id, p.category, p.name\n),\nranked AS (\n    SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY revenue DESC, name) AS rn\n    FROM product_revenue\n)\nSELECT category, name, revenue FROM ranked WHERE rn &lt;= 2 ORDER BY category, rn;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>category    | name       | revenue\n------------+------------+--------\nElectronics | Laptop     | 107000\nElectronics | Headphones | 10000\nFurniture   | Desk       | 16000\nFurniture   | Chair      | 13500\nStationery  | Notebook   | 1500\nStationery  | Pen        | 300</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Latest order per customer (with details)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT c.name, o.order_id, o.order_date, o.status\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE o.order_date = (SELECT MAX(order_date) FROM orders o2 WHERE o2.customer_id = c.customer_id)\nORDER BY c.customer_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | order_id | order_date | status\n-------+----------+------------+----------\nAnanya | 108      | 2024-03-15 | shipped\nRahul  | 106      | 2024-03-01 | delivered\nSneha  | 107      | 2024-03-08 | pending\nImran  | 105      | 2024-02-20 | cancelled</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you get the top 3 rows per group?</summary><div><p><code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> (exactly 3) or <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> (top 3 values incl. ties) <code>OVER (PARTITION BY group ORDER BY metric DESC)</code> in a CTE/subquery, then <code>WHERE rn &lt;= 3</code>. Alternatives: <code>LATERAL</code>/<code>CROSS APPLY</code> with <code>LIMIT 3</code> per group (PostgreSQL/SQL Server) — efficient with an index on (group, metric).</p></div></details></section>"}